  ConformanceDomain {
    key: "conformance-decision",
    label: "Decision / selection",
    question: "When this change chooses among candidates under objectives and constraints, does it inhabit the corpus's decision and Pareto models -- a DecisionSubject, a candidate field of ParetoEntry rows over declared SelectionAxis identities, a RealizationSelectionResult whose SelectedWithin arm binds a SelectionReceipt -- and where it uses another representation or another choosing route, is that departure and its reason stated?",
    tells: [
      "a locally authored candidate-selection result (a chosen/none/needs-more sum, a ranked list, a best-of fold) beside RealizationSelectionResult",
      "a parallel dominance, orientation, interval or evidence-gap representation where std.pareto already carries DominanceVerdict, AxisGoal and AxisGap",
      "a local convention for candidate-field identity, funded-axis identity or policy identity where SelectionReceipt already names them as declaration references",
      "a new choosing route that neither consumes select_realization nor states, beside itself, why it does not",
      "urgency, priority or a deadline weight authored as a local number, queue order or tier rather than as a declared time valuation (a std.measure money rate) on the demand or supply position whose owner holds it",
      "a declared valuation and an estimated consequence carried in one number, so a new observation can silently rewrite policy -- or a missing estimate read as zero, where zero is a declaration and unknown is not",
      "one cash consequence counted twice, once as cost and again as urgency (a rental's continuing charge restated as supply urgency)",
      "a deadline or availability bound priced as a very large valuation instead of held as a hard constraint",
      "an engine-internal choice among admissible execution alternatives (tracking grain, execution partition, fusion, batching, cache versus recompute, placement, specialization) made by a local threshold, ranked list, queue tier or cost rule that neither consumes select_realization nor states beside itself why that authority is inapplicable; a mechanism is such a choice when it selects among admitted alternatives that differ in obligations, constraints, funded objective axes or observable consequences (a FIFO pop order that decides which of differently valued work waits is a priority policy), while canonicalization, protocol-mandated order and mechanically equivalent internal choices remain implementation unless evidence or policy makes their difference material"
